  <h2>Thesis statement</h2>
  <p>In a post-quantum world characterized by AI agents autonomously executing decisions on behalf of individuals, the agentic economy will rely on decentralized, privacy-preserving infrastructures where individuals maintain sovereignty over their identity and data. This thesis argues that integrating self-sovereign identity (SSI), zero-knowledge cryptography, tokenized data vaults, and verifiable agent credentials will be essential to ensure personal data monetization, privacy, and trust at scale.</p>

  <h2>Core arguments</h2>
  <h3>1. Agentic economy equals AI first infrastructure</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>AI agents will represent humans and organizations autonomously in marketplaces, communications, and services.</li>
    <li>These agents must be identifiable, verifiable, and bound to human owners via SSI-based credential systems.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Implication:</em> A robust, decentralized identity framework for both humans and AI agents is critical to secure transactions and trust.</p>
  <h3>2. Privacy by design through SSI and ZKPs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Quantum-era threats require post-quantum cryptography (PQC) and zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) to ensure privacy resilience.</li>
    <li>Users must retain control over which attributes they disclose via selective disclosure and privacy-preserving credential proofs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Implication:</em> ZKPs and PQC-enabled wallets will be core enablers of a privacy-first identity infrastructure.</p>
  <h3>3. Personal data monetization via agent delegation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Tokenized personal data vaults will allow individuals to selectively license access to their data.</li>
    <li>Personal AI agents can act on behalf of users in data marketplaces, negotiating terms, enforcing consent via smart contracts, and earning rewards.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Implication:</em> Individuals can derive recurring micro-income streams while maintaining data sovereignty.</p>
  <h3>4. Decentralized credential economies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Credential issuers (governments, universities, enterprises) and verifiers (employers, platforms) will participate in decentralized marketplaces where credentials are monetized.</li>
    <li>Tokenized payment and reputation systems will underpin this infrastructure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Implication:</em> The value of verified information will be reflected in market mechanisms governed by token-based micro-transactions.</p>
  <h3>5. Quantum resistant identity ecosystems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Quantum computing threatens classical cryptographic systems underpinning identity and data infrastructures.</li>
    <li>Identity providers must transition to PQC (e.g., NIST-approved lattice cryptography) to maintain future-proof security.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Implication:</em> Providers offering quantum-safe upgrades and AI-ready credentialing services will gain early-mover advantage.</p>

  <h2>Strategic opportunities for innovation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Agent identity infrastructure.</strong> A SaaS or open-source platform that issues and manages verifiable credentials for AI agents (chatbots, drones, bots).</li>
    <li><strong>Post-quantum SSI wallet.</strong> A cross-platform wallet with integrated PQC, biometric backup, and agent delegation capabilities.</li>
    <li><strong>Data monetization co-ops.</strong> DAO-governed platforms that aggregate user data and sell anonymized insights with cryptographic guarantees.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy ad AI marketplaces.</strong> Agent-mediated, zero-knowledge ad networks where users get paid to opt in to personalized advertising.</li>
    <li><strong>Compliance middleware.</strong> Tooling that enables legacy enterprises to plug into decentralized identity systems without overhauling their stack.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Research contributions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bridging AI, blockchain, and quantum-resilient infrastructure to empower individual data agency.</li>
    <li>Proposing interoperable trust models and marketplaces where agents act on human-aligned incentives.</li>
    <li>Framing data as labor and identity as a programmable, monetizable asset.</li>
  </ul>
</Thesis>
